  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach of using SLSQP with local perturbations may not explore the global minimum effectively due to the problem's non-convexity. We propose a more efficient exploration strategy combining random sampling with optimization:\n\n- Use a deterministic initialization from `initial_h_values` and add random perturbations.\n- Employ an improved feasible region projection method.\n- Optimize each candidate using a constrained optimization method.\n- Track the best solution within the time budget.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe minimize the max cross-correlation over all shifts, represented as:\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\left(1 - \\sum_{i} h[i] h[i + k] \\cdot dx\\right)$$\nsubject to:\n$$\\sum h[i] = 35, \\quad h[i] \\in [0, 1]$$\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nSteps:\n1. Validate budget and initialize variables.\n2. Load or generate initial h.\n3. Project h onto feasible region:\n   a. Clip each h[i] to [0,1].\n   b. Scale clipped h to sum to 35.\n4. Evaluate initial h.\n5. Generate 100 candidate perturbations:\n   a. Randomly select 10% of points.\n   b. Apply Gaussian perturbations.\n   c. Project to feasibility.\n   d. Optimize each candidate using SLSQP with tighter tolerances.\n6. Track the best solution.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Perturbation strength: 0.1\n- Random points: 10% of 70\n- Optimization iterations: 150\n- Choices: substitution varies to maintain feasibility\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each run takes ~30s\n- Handle feasibility via arithmetic checks\n- Ensure termination within 1100s\n- Return best feasible solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure sum_within_tol, elements_within_bounds\n- Compute C5 using given objective",
